+# The Daily Grind
+
+A responsive coffee account experience with passwordless WebAuthn registration, authentication, passkey management, and reauthentication.
+
+## Run
+
+Requires Node.js 22.13+ (Node 24 recommended) and pnpm.
+
+```sh
+pnpm install
+pnpm start
+```
+
+Open `http://localhost:11222`. Passkeys require HTTPS outside localhost. Create an account with your name, email, and a passkey, or sign in using an existing discoverable passkey. There is no password or seeded account.
+
+## Deployment
+
+- `PORT`: server port, defaults to `11222`.
+- `APP_ORIGIN`: exact public origin, for example `https://coffee.example.com`. Set this in production so verification uses a fixed trusted origin and relying-party ID. Local development defaults to the request host and protocol.
+- `DATA_DIR`: SQLite database directory, defaults to `./data`. Mount persistent storage here. Database files contain account information and public credential material; never serve this directory publicly.
+- Serve through HTTPS. If using a reverse proxy, configure it to overwrite the forwarded protocol header.
+- Keep a stable hostname: passkeys are bound to the site’s relying-party ID.
+
+Sessions use random, HttpOnly, SameSite cookies, expire after seven days, and rotate after authentication. HTTPS enables Secure cookies. Challenges are single-use and expire after five minutes. Changes to passkeys require authentication within the last five minutes. The last passkey cannot be removed until another has been added.
+
+Accounts use an email as an identifier; this app does not verify email ownership or offer email-based account recovery. Encourage users to add a backup passkey or use a synced credential. The current backend is designed for a single Node process; challenges and request rate limits are in memory. Use a shared challenge/rate-limit store before deploying multiple instances.
+
+## Automated checks
+
+```sh
+pnpm test
+```
+
+The integration test uses generated P-256 keys to exercise actual attestation and assertion verification without opening a browser. It covers the full lifecycle, required user verification, challenge replay, wrong origins, signature tampering, account isolation, freshness gates, last-passkey protection, session logout, and static asset delivery. Device and browser prompt behavior still depends on the client platform.
